import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import LiveLink from '../../components/LiveLink.astro';

const facts = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Product Design Lead' },
  { label: 'Org', value: 'Discovery Education' },
  { label: 'Product', value: 'Experience' },
  { label: 'Partners', value: 'Product · Engineering · Curriculum · Analytics · Marketing' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="I don't know where I am · Jessica Loredo"
  description="Rebuilding a K-12 learning platform around teacher workflow: product identity, information architecture, and a taxonomy that encodes how instruction sequences."
  eyebrow="Case study · Experience Transformation"
  headline="“I don’t know where I am”"
  lede="Two products shared one front door, and the single most common search on the platform was no search at all."
  facts={facts}
>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the finding</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>The most-run query was empty</h2>
      <p>
        Teachers logged in, landed on the home page, went straight to search, and pressed enter
        without typing anything. Not occasionally. <strong>An empty query was the most common
        search on the platform.</strong> The second most common was &ldquo;magic school bus.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        An empty search isn't a search. It's someone looking for a place to stand. Teachers were
        using the search results page as a landing page, because the actual landing page hadn't
        told them where they were or what they could do there. They skimmed past it, since it wasn't
        what they were looking for, and kept going until they hit a screen that at least showed
        them the shape of the library.
      </p>
      <p>
        That reading matched what teachers said out loud. Across interviews and field feedback, one
        complaint came back more than any other.
      </p>
      <PullQuote attribution="the most consistent piece of user feedback we had">
        I don't know where I am.
      </PullQuote>
      <p>
        Behavioral data and stated frustration were describing the same failure from two
        directions. That framing is what made the problem tractable: this wasn't a search-relevance
        problem or a content problem. It was an orientation problem.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the problem</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Two products, one front door</h2>
      <p>
        The platform home and the Experience home had converged into effectively the same surface.
        Curriculum packs, tools, subject entry points, and product tiles all competed on one page,
        and nothing on it answered the question a teacher actually arrives with: <em>what do I do
        next?</em>
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="Before: the platform home and the Experience home had become the same page, with product tiles, curriculum packs, and tools competing for the same attention."
        needs="Before-state screenshots: the old DE home page and the old Experience home side by side."
      />
      <p>
        Underneath the layout problem was a harder one. Ask a teacher what <em>Experience</em> was
        and they couldn't tell you. It was all just &ldquo;DE.&rdquo; The product had no identity
        distinct from the platform it sat on, which meant it couldn't be described by sales,
        couldn't be found by users, and couldn't be measured by anyone.
      </p>
      <p>The symptoms showed up everywhere at once:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Teachers couldn't find content matched to their grade, subject, and teaching context</li>
        <li>&ldquo;I didn't know we had that feature&rdquo; was a routine response in research</li>
        <li>No clear answer to what came next after finding a resource</li>
        <li>Sales couldn't tell a coherent story about where the product fit</li>
        <li>Internally, nobody agreed on the boundary between Experience and the platform</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">constraints</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>What made it hard</h2>
      <p>
        This was a live product with a decade of habit built on top of it, mid-migration between
        design systems, owned by no single team.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Legacy and new coexisting.</strong> The product was a mix of legacy platform
          pages and newer design system pages, so any change had to work in both worlds at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The school calendar.</strong> Teacher habits are seasonal and releases are too.
          You cannot rearrange a teacher's room in October. Large-scale change has to land when
          they're able to absorb it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Identity was a shared decision.</strong> Experience's identity couldn't be
          defined without the platform's identity being defined alongside it, and that sat with a
          different team.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inherited work.</strong> A prior third-party UX engagement had already produced
          direction. My job started with continuing and translating that work rather than
          restarting it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Moving org.</strong> Team and ownership changes ran through the whole effort.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">framing</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>The question we agreed to answer</h2>
      <p>
        I ran a series of cross-functional workshops across design, product, engineering,
        curriculum, analytics, and marketing to get to one problem statement everyone would
        actually sign up for.
        The output mattered less than the alignment: for the first time the teams had shared
        language for what we were solving.
      </p>
      <PullQuote>
        How might we design an educator experience that makes finding the right content fast and
        intuitive, personalizes to each teacher's grade, subject, and region, simplifies navigation
        through a consistent interface, and communicates the product's role in the broader
        ecosystem, all while respecting how little time teachers have?
      </PullQuote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the model</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>A model of the work, not a map of the org</h2>
      <p>
        Products drift toward mirroring the company that builds them. The fix was to anchor the
        architecture in what teachers do instead, and to name it in four stages we could all point
        at: <strong>Discover, Plan, Deliver, Reflect.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        This gave us something we hadn't had: a test. When a debate started about whether some
        capability belonged to Experience or to the platform, we could ask which stage of the
        teacher's work it served. Ownership arguments that had run for months became answerable in
        a meeting.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="Information architecture v1.0: the platform layer, the Experience workflow layered on top of it, and the four stages of teacher work below."
        needs="The IA v1.0 diagram (portfolio deck, p.12)."
        ratio="16 / 10"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">taxonomy</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Organizing by instructional purpose</h2>
      <p>
        The piece I'd defend hardest is the taxonomy. Content had been organized the way a library
        is organized, by what a thing <em>is</em>. A video, an activity, a lesson, a quiz. But a
        teacher planning tomorrow morning isn't looking for a video. They're looking for something
        to open with.
      </p>
      <p>So topics are banded by the job the content does in a lesson:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Activate Thinking</strong>: get them in the door</li>
        <li><strong>Build Knowledge</strong>: teach the thing</li>
        <li><strong>Practice &amp; Review</strong>: make it stick</li>
        <li><strong>Apply &amp; Extend</strong>: push it further</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Those bands are a teaching model, not navigation categories. The information architecture
        mirrors the sequence of instruction itself, which means a teacher scanning a topic page is
        reading something already shaped like their lesson plan. Combined with a real hierarchy of
        subject, then topic, then resource, a page could finally answer &ldquo;where am
        I?&rdquo; without the teacher having to ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        The clearest way to see it is on a single topic. For a sixth-grade unit on force, the four
        bands line up as a car-collision video to open with, an explainer to teach from, an
        interactive to practice against, and a student investigation to extend into. One complete
        lesson arc, readable in a single row.
      </p>
      <LiveLink
        href="https://nuxt-experience-proto.vercel.app/"
        label="Experience home, prototyped"
        note="Built in Nuxt to stress-test the model: personalization, the instructional-strategy bands, and continuity through Jump Back In."
      />
      <Figure
        caption="After: subject and topic hubs, with resources banded by instructional strategy rather than content type."
        needs="Shipped screenshots: the Science subject hub, the Life Science topic hub, and a topic page showing the four instructional-strategy bands."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the loop</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Closing the loop between teachers and students</h2>
      <p>
        A workflow product that stops at &ldquo;assigned&rdquo; is only half a product. I mapped
        both sides as one system: the teacher's Discover → Plan → Deliver → Reflect running above,
        the student's Orient → Engage → Reflect running below, and a return path where what
        students actually did with an assignment feeds back into the teacher's next planning cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        That return path is the argument. It's what makes the teacher's reflect stage real instead
        of decorative, it's what makes impact visible to district leaders, and it demonstrates that
        the product is designed as a whole rather than a set of fragments.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="The two-lane ecosystem map: teacher and student journeys with the engagement data return path."
        needs="The teacher/student journey map (CoTeach ecosystem diagram)."
        ratio="16 / 9"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">measurement</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Defining a word everyone used and nobody had defined</h2>
      <p>
        Across my time in edtech I've listened to a lot of product leaders ask their teams for
        &ldquo;engaging&rdquo; experiences without examining what that actually requires. Over the
        past decade the word became a catch-all for badges, avatars, and streak mechanics. Shallow
        features standing in for design that meets a student where they are. Some of those
        mechanics genuinely do hold attention, but attention is not learning. You can capture one
        without ever earning the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Engagement isn't really a product feature. It's a psychological state, and cultivating it
        means sitting with a person, listening, attuning, and seeking genuine understanding. That is
        a harder thing to ask a roadmap for, which is exactly why it gets replaced with points.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which brought us here. Leadership had one non-negotiable: whatever we built had to be
        measurable. But &ldquo;engagement&rdquo; was being used to mean six different things
        depending on who was talking, and the platform's existing proxy was completion, which
        measures compliance and calls it learning.
      </p>
      <p>So I wrote the definition down, starting with what it isn't:</p>
      <PullQuote>
        We are not saying engagement is learning. We are saying engagement helps answer <em>why</em>
        learning sometimes fails or succeeds when content quality is held constant.
      </PullQuote>
      <p>
        From there, six component measures (engagement, progress, exploration, learning
        expression, social experience, and social-emotional engagement) composing into a single
        index. Exploration counts optional content and time in non-required material, because
        curiosity is a signal. Learning expression is reflection-driven: one thing I learned, one
        question I still have.
      </p>
      <p>
        The index answers one question: <strong>how deeply did students interact with
        teacher-delivered learning experiences?</strong> Each persona gets their own version of it.
        District leaders ask how embedded the product is in instruction, teachers ask whether
        students engaged with what they intended, students ask whether it was worth their time.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="The six component scores and how they compose into a single index."
        needs="The measurement framework diagram (portfolio deck, p.15)."
        ratio="16 / 9"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">iteration</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Three passes to get here</h2>
      <p>
        The direction inherited from the prior third-party engagement was a starting point, not an
        answer. We went through three major design iterations before landing where the product is
        now. Each one stress-tested the model against real teacher workflows and against what
        engineering could actually deliver inside the release calendar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rapid prototyping did most of the work of settling arguments. Rather than debating concepts
        in the abstract, I built them, using AI tooling to get from idea to working prototype fast
        enough that a concept could be argued about on a screen in the same week it was proposed.
        A bundling interaction let teachers collect resources while browsing instead of breaking
        flow to go somewhere else. Asset cards were reworked so a teacher can tell what they're
        looking at in under two seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Being able to hand a team something clickable changed the shape of the conversation.
        Objections that would have taken a month of meetings to surface arrived in an afternoon,
        which is the entire point.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="Rapid prototypes used to stress-test the resource bundling concept and the asset card hierarchy."
        needs="Prototype screenshots (portfolio deck, p.18)."
        ratio="16 / 10"
      />
      <p>
        Within two to three weeks of the first workshop, the effect was less about artifacts than
        about momentum: engineering, product, analytics, curriculum, design, marketing, and
        leadership working from one mental model, decisions moving faster, and the harder
        conversations about risk and tradeoffs finally happening in the open.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">where it landed</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        Experience and the platform now have separate homes, and Experience has a real one: a page
        built around what a teacher came to do rather than a directory of everything the company
        sells. Content sits in a clear hierarchy, banded by instructional purpose. Navigation was
        rebuilt against the four-stage model.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Field feedback so far is that teachers are responding well to the new hierarchy and
        taxonomy</strong>, the two things most directly aimed at &ldquo;I don't know where I
        am.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <h3>Where it breaks, and why I expected it to</h3>
      <p>
        Teachers are getting tripped up at the asset player, the page you land on when you
        actually open a resource. It doesn't share the visual language or the navigation of the
        surfaces around it, so the orientation we rebuilt everywhere else drops away at the exact
        moment a teacher commits to a piece of content.
      </p>
      <p>
        I raised this as a risk during design, and it's where the friction is showing up now. It's
        also the clearest illustration of the constraint I started with: <strong>a product's
        identity can only be as coherent as the platform it sits inside.</strong> The player is a
        shared surface. Fixing that seam isn't a design decision, it's a cross-team one, which
        makes it an argument to keep making rather than a problem to solve alone.
      </p>
      <h3>Also still open</h3>
      <p>
        The product's identity is still forming; one release doesn't undo years of being
        indistinguishable from the platform it runs on. The navigation is better than it was and
        looser than I want it, for the same shared-surface reason. And the work surfaced several
        other areas that need attention, which is what happens when you can finally see a product
        clearly enough to evaluate it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next argument is the student side, where the same thinking hasn't been applied yet.
        That's <a href="/work">a separate piece of work</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
